{% sec 1, "A Price on Uncertainty", "What decides how wide a market maker's quote should be?", "A price on uncertainty" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think about a builder pricing a fixed-price job. For a new extension on clear ground, the quote is the materials and labour plus a small margin. For an old house where nobody knows what's behind the walls, the quote is much higher, because the builder adds a contingency for what might go wrong. The builder isn't being greedy. The extra is the price of the uncertainty they're agreeing to carry.</p>
    <p>Deltashark says the width of a market maker's quote is exactly that: <strong>a price on uncertainty</strong>, and its minimum is a {% gl "spread floor" %}floor{% endgl %}. Every part of it maps to a risk or a cost, and that explains a pattern seen in every market: spreads widen when volatility rises, when some traders know more than others, and when flow thins out.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">The half-spread floor (Deltashark)</span>\[ \frac{s}{2} \;\ge\; \underbrace{q\,m}_{\text{adverse selection}} \;+\; \underbrace{c_{\text{inv}}}_{\text{inventory cost per fill}} \;+\; \underbrace{c_{\text{op}}}_{\text{fees and costs}} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Each fill has to expect to cover its toll to {% gl "informed flow" %}informed traders{% endgl %} (The Free Option), its share of the cost of carrying {% gl "inventory" %}inventory{% endgl %} until it's passed on (Inventory Risk), and explicit costs such as exchange fees. Competition pushes the half-spread down towards this floor from above. The inventory term grows with volatility over the expected holding time, \(\sigma\sqrt{h}\), so anything that makes prices jumpier or flow slower raises the floor. In fast markets both happen at once (volatility jumps and flow turns one-sided), which is why quoted spreads gap wider in stress: <strong>the floor moved, and the quotes moved with it</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example (Deltashark): repricing the quote for a volatile hour</span>
    <h3>Normally: informed toll 1 tick, inventory cost 0.5, fees 0.5, so a 2-tick half-spread floor and a 4-wide quote. Ahead of a scheduled release, the informed share doubles the toll to 2 and volatility doubles the inventory term to 1. What should the maker quote?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>New floor: \(s/2 \ge 2 + 1 + 0.5 = 3.5\) ticks.</li>
      <li>So \(s \ge 7\) ticks, not 4.</li>
      <li>A maker that keeps quoting 4 wide through the release is selling underpriced options to informed traders.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">The right quote is nearly twice as wide, or no quote at all. Wide spreads around information events aren't liquidity failing; they're liquidity correctly priced.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Price the width</h3><p>Build the half-spread floor from its three parts. Pick a market condition, then adjust the parts. The marker shows a quote of 4 ticks wide (2 each side) for comparison.</p></div>
