{% sec 2, "Above the Floor", "If the floor is the minimum, how wide should a market maker actually quote?", "Above the floor" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Once the floor is covered, width becomes a revenue decision. Earning the Spread showed the hump: wider quotes earn more per fill but fill less often, because some traders won't pay more and competitors' tighter quotes catch the flow first. Expected revenue per hour is margin times fill rate, and the maker's width decision is a continual search for the peak as conditions move the curve around.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example (Deltashark): choosing between two widths</span>
    <h3>At 4 ticks wide: 30 fills an hour, net margin after tolls 0.5 ticks a fill. At 6 wide: 18 fills, net margin 1.5. Which is better?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>4 wide: \(30 \times 0.5 = 15\) ticks an hour.</li>
      <li>6 wide: \(18 \times 1.5 = 27\) ticks an hour.</li>
      <li>The wider quote wins despite fewer fills: near the floor, margin is so thin that volume can't make up for it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">The comparison flips when the floor is low and flow is very price-sensitive, which is the everyday state of liquid markets. There's no universally correct width, only the current peak of margin times volume.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>With Earning the Spread's model of fills falling away exponentially (\(N = A e^{-\kappa s}\)), the peak has a neat form. Net revenue is \((s/2 - f)\,A e^{-\kappa s}\), where \(f\) is the floor, and it's highest at:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">The best width above a floor</span>\[ s^{*} = 2f + \frac{1}{\kappa} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The best quote is <strong>the floor, plus a markup set by competition</strong>. In crowded markets, flow is very price-sensitive (\(\kappa\) is large), so the markup is tiny and quotes sit barely above the floor. In thin markets with few rivals (\(\kappa\) small), the markup can be large. Deltashark draws the conclusion: that's why spreads vary more across instruments than any one cost does.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "The Full Avellaneda–Stoikov Model", "What does the standard model say about width and centre together?", "Avellaneda–Stoikov" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Quote Skewing introduced half of the {% gl "avellaneda–stoikov model" %}Avellaneda–Stoikov model{% endgl %}: the {% gl "indifference price" %}indifference price{% endgl %} that sets the centre. The other half sets the total width of the quote around it:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Avellaneda–Stoikov (2008): centre and width</span>\[ r = s - q\,\gamma\,\sigma^{2}(T - t) \qquad \delta^{a} + \delta^{b} = \gamma\,\sigma^{2}(T - t) + \frac{2}{\gamma}\ln\!\left(1 + \frac{\gamma}{\kappa}\right) \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The bid and offer sit around \(r\), a total of \(\delta^{a} + \delta^{b}\) apart. The width has two parts, which match this note's two sections:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>\(\gamma\sigma^{2}(T - t)\): the inventory part.</strong> More risk aversion, more volatility or more time left to carry positions means a wider quote. It's this model's version of \(c_{\text{inv}}\).</li>
      <li><strong>\(\tfrac{2}{\gamma}\ln(1 + \gamma/\kappa)\): the competition part.</strong> It depends on \(\kappa\), how quickly fills fall away with distance. For a maker who barely minds risk (\(\gamma\) close to zero), it becomes \(2/\kappa\): exactly the markup above, written per side (\(1/\kappa\) on each).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>What the model leaves out is the informed trader: it has no {% gl "adverse selection" %}adverse selection{% endgl %} term. Real desks add one, which is the \(qm\) part of Deltashark's floor. Avellaneda–Stoikov is the starting point for most market making research and for many crypto market making bots, so it's worth knowing well for interviews.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example</span>
    <h3>\(\gamma = 0.1\), \(\kappa = 1.5\) per tick, and \(\sigma^{2}(T - t) = 2\) ticks². What's the optimal total width?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Inventory part: \(0.1 \times 2 = 0.2\) ticks.</li>
      <li>Competition part: \(\tfrac{2}{0.1}\ln(1 + 0.1/1.5) = 20 \times \ln(1.0667) \approx 20 \times 0.0645 \approx 1.29\) ticks.</li>
      <li>Total: \(\approx 1.49\) ticks, centred on the indifference price.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Here competition sets most of the width. Double the volatility (so \(\sigma^{2}\) quadruples) and the inventory part grows to \(0.8\), for a total of about \(2.1\) ticks.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "Don't Copy the Competitor", "If someone quotes tighter than you, should you match them?", "Don't copy the competitor" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Deltashark's trap for this lesson: matching a competitor's tighter quote is only right if their floor really is lower. They might requote faster, see cheaper flow, or have a better fair value model. It's wrong if they're simply mispricing the risk. And being undercut doesn't always mean you're wrong. <strong>Width should come from your own measured tolls, your own recycling speed and your own costs, then be adjusted for competition</strong>, not copied from it.</p>
    <p>The way to lower your floor is to work on its parts: faster requoting cuts the informed toll (The Free Option's markouts), better flow cuts it further (client tiering), quicker recycling cuts the inventory cost (Quote Skewing), and scale cuts the cost per fill. That's the business case for everything a market making firm invests in.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

