  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember from the chapter</span>
    <p>A market maker sets two numbers after every fill: where to centre its quotes (inventory and fair value) and how wide to make them (information, volatility, holding time and competition). Everything else, from speed to client tiering to hard limits, exists to make those two numbers cheaper to get right.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "And in FX, Gold and Bonds?", "When do spreads widen in my three markets?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>FX</h3>
    <p>FX spreads follow the floor around the clock. They're tightest when London and New York overlap, wider in the quiet hours, and widest at the Sunday reopening, on holidays and around big data. The clearest example of the floor jumping is the yen flash event of 3 January 2019. In the thin gap between the New York close and the Asian open, with Japan on holiday, the yen jumped against the dollar in minutes. The Reserve Bank of Australia reported bid-ask spreads widening from around 2 {% gl "pip" %}pips{% endgl %} to 100–300 pips. Market makers weren't failing: with almost no flow and huge uncertainty, the floor really was that high.</p>
    <h3>Gold</h3>
    <p>Gold spreads widen around US data and Fed decisions, at the Asian open, and in stress, when the inventory cost rises because bullion is harder to move between London, New York and Asia (as in March 2020).</p>
    <h3>Bonds</h3>
    <p>Benchmark government bonds have tight spreads in normal times, but they widen around inflation data and central bank meetings, and they blew out in March 2020 when dealers' balance sheets filled up. Corporate bonds have far wider spreads all the time: the holding time is days rather than seconds, so the inventory term is large, and some clients know more about the company's credit than the dealer.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Wide</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A large spread.</p><p class="eg">"Market's wide into the number."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Tight</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A small spread.</p><p class="eg">"It's a penny tight all day in the front month."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Pull</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Cancel your quotes and stop making a market for a while.</p><p class="eg">"Pull everything thirty seconds before payrolls."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Back in</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Quoting again after pulling.</p><p class="eg">"We're back in, two wide."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Size down</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Show smaller quantities on your quotes.</p><p class="eg">"Size down, the flow's getting toxic."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Floor</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The narrowest spread that still covers your costs and risks.</p><p class="eg">"We're quoting right on the floor in that name."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Gap</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A sudden widening, or a jump in price with nothing trading in between.</p><p class="eg">"Spreads gapped out on the headline."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Thin</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Little flow and little size in the book.</p><p class="eg">"It's thin over the holiday, keep it wide."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where spread width turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: when your costs jump</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Your broker's spread follows the same floor, so plan your trading around it. Trading EUR/USD or gold in the London–New York overlap is cheap; trading at the Sunday open, on a holiday or in the minute after a big release can cost many times as much. The January 2019 yen event is also a warning about stop orders: in a thin market, a stop becomes a market order into a book with 100-pip spreads. If you trade around news, size for the spread you'll actually get, not the one on a quiet afternoon.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: why some places have wide spreads</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>In property, the "spread" is the gap between what a quick-sale buyer pays and what a patient seller gets. It's wide where the floor is high: unusual or holiday properties (long holding time, few buyers), areas where values are uncertain, and markets where the other side may know more (a local seller against a foreign buyer). Pricing villas means estimating that floor for each location: how long a unit will sit, how much prices could move meanwhile, and how much the buyer knows that you don't.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: pricing naira in a storm</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A naira–dollar app's spread should follow the floor too: tighter on quiet days, wider before CBN announcements and in weeks when the naira is moving fast, when informed users (those who know about a policy change or a big payment) are more likely and holding currency inventory is riskier. Copying a rival's rate is Deltashark's trap: if the rival is underpricing the risk, matching them means sharing their losses. Building the spread from measured costs per conversion is the sounder way to price.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 10, "The Maths Underneath", "How do the floor, the peak and Avellaneda–Stoikov fit together?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">The floor, with an inventory cost proportional to \(\sigma\sqrt{h}\)</span>\[ f = q\,m + \lambda\,\sigma\sqrt{h} + c_{\text{op}} \qquad s_{\min} = 2f \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Net revenue above the floor, and its peak</span>\[ \Pi(s) = \left(\frac{s}{2} - f\right) A e^{-\kappa s} \qquad \frac{d\Pi}{ds} = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \frac{s^{*}}{2} - f = \frac{1}{2\kappa} \;\Rightarrow\; s^{*} = 2f + \frac{1}{\kappa} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>\(\lambda\) is how much the desk charges per unit of risk held; in the Price the width interactive it's \(0.5\), so normal conditions (\(\sigma = 1\) tick a minute, \(h = 1\) minute) give Deltashark's \(0.5\)-tick inventory cost. The peak formula comes from differentiating \(\Pi(s)\): \(\tfrac{1}{2}e^{-\kappa s} - \kappa(\tfrac{s}{2} - f)e^{-\kappa s} = 0\).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example</span>
    <h3>The floor is 2 ticks a side and \(\kappa = 0.5\) per tick. What's the best width? And if a new competitor makes the flow twice as price-sensitive?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>\(s^{*} = 2 \times 2 + 1/0.5 = 4 + 2 = 6\) ticks.</li>
      <li>With \(\kappa = 1\): \(s^{*} = 4 + 1 = 5\) ticks.</li>
      <li>Competition removed a tick of markup, but it can't take the quote below the 4-tick floor.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Competition squeezes the markup; only better technology, flow or models can lower the floor itself.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 11, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer. These double as interview questions." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>(Deltashark quick check) Informed toll \(1.5\) ticks per fill, inventory cost \(1\) tick, fees \(0.5\). What is the minimum full spread in ticks?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Half-spread \(\ge 1.5 + 1 + 0.5 = 3\), so the full spread is at least \(6\) ticks.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>(Deltashark quick check) Width A: \(40\) fills an hour at \(0.4\) ticks net margin. Width B: \(25\) fills an hour at \(0.8\). Which earns more per hour, and how many ticks does the better one earn?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>A: \(40 \times 0.4 = 16\). B: \(25 \times 0.8 = 20\). B earns more, \(20\) ticks an hour.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>A maker's inventory cost per fill is \(0.5\) ticks in normal conditions. Volatility doubles and holding time quadruples. What's the new inventory cost, if it scales with \(\sigma\sqrt{h}\)?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(0.5 \times 2 \times \sqrt{4} = 2\) ticks per fill.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>The floor is \(1.5\) ticks a side and \(\kappa = 2\). What's the best width?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(s^{*} = 2 \times 1.5 + 1/2 = 3.5\) ticks.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>In Avellaneda–Stoikov, what happens to the optimal width when the market maker becomes very relaxed about risk (\(\gamma \to 0\))?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>The inventory part \(\gamma\sigma^{2}(T - t)\) goes to zero and the competition part \(\tfrac{2}{\gamma}\ln(1 + \gamma/\kappa)\) tends to \(2/\kappa\). The width is then set purely by how price-sensitive the flow is.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>Interview question: why do spreads widen before major economic announcements?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Because the floor rises. The chance that the next trader knows something (or reacts faster) goes up, so the adverse selection toll rises; volatility is about to jump, so the cost of holding any inventory rises; and some makers pull quotes, so flow is thinner and holding times longer. A maker quoting its normal width would be selling cheap options to whoever trades first after the news. Wider spreads are liquidity correctly priced.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">7</span><span>Interview question: you're quoting a stock and a competitor consistently shows one tick tighter. What do you do?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>First, work out whether their floor is really lower. I'd look at my own markouts and realised spreads: if my flow is toxic at my current width, matching them would lose money. I'd check whether they're faster (my fills come when the market moves), whether they see better flow, or whether their fair value is better. If their floor is genuinely lower, I'd work on mine (speed, models, flow) rather than just copying the number. If they're mispricing, I'd let them have the flow and watch whether they keep quoting there.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
